\p
\v 31 Then¦68044 Yahweh¦68048 sent a wind that blew quails¦68050 from¦68051 the¦68053 ocean¦68053, and¦68054 \add ≈they dropped\add* on the¦68057 ground¦68070 a day's walk in every direction around¦68064 the¦68065 camp¦68065 \add until they were piled\add* a metre high.
\v 32 \add ≈So¦68072\add* the¦68073 people¦68073 went out and¦68078 collected the¦68080 quail¦68088—\add ≈each¦68081 family getting at least¦68089 the¦68083 equivalent of¦68083 ten¦68091 large barrels full\add*, and¦68093 they¦68093 spread the meat around¦68096 the¦68097 camp¦68097 \add to sun dry\add*.
\v 33 Then when they started to \add cook and\add* eat \add some of the fresh\add* meat¦68099, Yahweh¦68106 \add ≈got very¦68114 angry¦68105\add* and¦68109 struck¦68109 the people¦68111 with a¦68112 terrible \add ≈deadly disease¦68112\add* before they could even chew¦68104 it¦68107.
\v 34 \add ≈So¦68116\add* that place¦68121 was named¦68119 Kivrot-Hattaavah¦68124 \add (which means ‘Graves of the cravers’)\add*, because¦68125 that was where they¦68128 buried¦68128 the¦68131 people¦68131 who¦68132 had craved¦68132 \add <meat\add*.
\v 35 From¦68134 Kivrot-Hattaavah, the¦68137 people¦68137 \add ≈moved on\add* to Hatserot¦68138 and¦68139 \add ≈stayed\add* \add *there¦68140\add*.
\c 12
\s1 Miryam's punishment
