\p
\v 1 \add ≈One day,\add* Miryam¦68144 and¦68145 Aharon¦68145 spoke¦68143 out against¦68146 Mosheh¦68146 because¦68154 he'd¦68153 taken¦68153 an \add Ethiopian\add* woman (Kushitess) as a¦68156 wife¦68156,
\v 2 and¦68160 they¦68160 \add ≈challenged\add*, “Did Yahweh really only speak through¦68164 Mosheh¦68164? Doesn't he¦68165 also¦68168 speak through¦68170 us¦68170?” Yahweh¦68173 heard¦68172 \add what they said\add*.
\v 3 (Now Mosheh¦68176 was¦68177 \add actually\add* a very¦68179 humble¦68177 man¦68175—more \add ≡humble¦68177\add* than¦68180 anyone else \add ≈alive\add*.)
\p
\v 4 Then¦68189 Yahweh¦68190 suddenly¦68191 told¦68189 Mosheh¦68194, Aharon¦68197, and¦68198 Miryam¦68200, “Go out¦68201, the¦68202 three¦68202 of¦68202 you¦68202, to¦68203 the¦68205 \add ≈sacred\add* tent¦68205.” \add ≈So¦68207\add* the¦68208 three¦68208 of¦68208 them¦68208 went¦68207 there.
\v 5 and¦68210 Yahweh¦68211 descended¦68210 in¦68212 a¦68212 pillar¦68212 of¦68212 cloud¦68213 and¦68214 stood¦68214 at the¦68216 tent¦68216 opening and¦68217 called¦68217 Aharon¦68218 and¦68219 Miryam¦68219, and¦68220 the two of¦68221 them¦68221 \add ≈stepped forward\add*,
